%I A401839 #7 Nov 11 2026 22:21:21
%S A401839 10,5,10,26,13,65,65,85,34,34,130,10,85,130,26,170,50,25,325,325,170,
%T A401839 58,145,17,25,145,290,85,145,370,185,481,74,74,130,442,1105,65,58,481,
%U A401839 50,410,82,3485,41,29,185,442,130,50,290,205,205,221,370,425,170,754
%N A401839 a(n) is the least positive integer k such that each side of the triangle with sides k*A120133(n), k*A120132(n), k*A120131(n) divides its area.
%C A401839 This sequence is well defined since multiplying the sides by their LCM always gives a triangle whose sides all divide its area.
%C A401839 The odd prime divisors of the terms are all congruent to 1 (mod 4), and no term is divisible by 4. Proof:
%C A401839 Let x, y, z be the sides of the primitive Heronian triangle, K its area, and L = lcm(x, y, z), so that a(n) = L/gcd(L, K) by the formula below. Let v_p denote the p-adic valuation. Heron's formula gives (4*K)^2 + (x^2 + y^2 - z^2)^2 = (2*x*y)^2.
%C A401839 In any integer Pythagorean triple A^2 + B^2 = C^2, if p = 2 or p is a prime congruent to 3 (mod 4), then v_p(A) >= v_p(C) and v_p(B) >= v_p(C). Indeed, p | C implies p | A and p | B: for odd p this follows because -1 is a quadratic nonresidue, and for p = 2 by reducing modulo 4. Repeated division by p proves the assertion.
%C A401839 Applied to the triple above, this gives v_p(4*K) >= v_p(2*x*y), and the same holds with any two of the three sides in place of x and y.
%C A401839 Now let p be a prime congruent to 3 (mod 4), and choose x to be a side for which e = v_p(x) is maximal. Then v_p(K) = v_p(4*K) >= v_p(2*x*y) >= e.
%C A401839 For p = 2, choose x with e = v_2(x) maximal. Then v_2(4*K) >= v_2(2*x*y) >= e + 1, giving v_2(K) >= e - 1.
%C A401839 Consequently, a(n) = L/gcd(L, K) has no prime factor congruent to 3 (mod 4), and v_2(a(n)) <= 1. Thus every term belongs to A008784.
%H A401839 Felix Huber, <a href="/A401839/b401839.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>.
%H A401839 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/HeronianTriangle.html">Heronian Triangle</a>.
%F A401839 a(n) = L/gcd(L, A072294(n)), where L = lcm(A120131(n), A120132(n), A120133(n)).
%e A401839 a(1) = 10: The triangle with sides 3, 4, 5 has area 6, but its sides do not all divide 6. For the triangle with sides 3*k, 4*k, 5*k and area 6*k^2, the side 3*k always divides the area, 4*k divides it if and only if k is even, and 5*k divides it if and only if 5 divides k. The least such k is 10, giving sides 30, 40, 50 and area 600, and each side divides 600.
%e A401839 a(4) = 26: The triangle with sides 5, 12, 13 has area 30. For the triangle with sides 5*k, 12*k, 13*k and area 30*k^2, the side 5*k always divides the area, 12*k divides it if and only if k is even, and 13*k divides it if and only if 13 divides k. The least such k is 26, giving sides 130, 312, 338 and area 20280, and each side divides 20280.
%p A401839 A401839 := proc(n)
%p A401839     local i, l, q, s, x, y, z;
%p A401839     i := 0;
%p A401839     for z from 1 do
%p A401839         for y from 1 to z do
%p A401839             for x from z - y + 2 to y by 2 do
%p A401839                 s := (x + y + z)/2;
%p A401839                 if igcd(x, y, z) = 1 then
%p A401839                     q := s*(s - x)*(s - y)*(s - z);
%p A401839                     if issqr(q) then
%p A401839                         i := i + 1;
%p A401839                         if i = n then
%p A401839                             l := ilcm(x, y, z);
%p A401839                             return l/igcd(l, isqrt(q));
%p A401839                         end if;
%p A401839                     end if;
%p A401839                 end if;
%p A401839             end do;
%p A401839         end do;
%p A401839     end do;
%p A401839 end proc:
%p A401839 seq(A401839(n), n = 1 .. 58);
%Y A401839 Cf. A008784, A072294, A120131, A120132, A120133, A401840, A401841.
%K A401839 nonn,easy,new
%O A401839 1,1
%A A401839 _Felix Huber_, Nov 07 2026
